!Series_title	"Analysis of gene expression profiles in matched cancer-associated and non-tumoral fibroblasts from patients with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma"
!Series_geo_accession	"GSE303690"
!Series_status	"Public on Jun 22 2026"
!Series_submission_date	"Jul 25 2025"
!Series_last_update_date	"Jun 22 2026"
!Series_summary	"Among stromal components, cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs) constitute the predominant non-malignant population in the HNSCC microenvironment, where their abundance correlates with advanced disease and poor prognosis. CAFs support tumor progression via extracellular matrix remodeling and paracrine signaling through cytokines, growth factors, exosomes and miRNA. Here, we carried out a transcriptomic analysis of 6 couples of CAFs and matching non-tumoral fibroblasts (NFs) derived from HNSCC patients in order to reveal gene expression changes between CAF and NF cell populations and identify potential CAF-specific therapeutic targets."
!Series_overall_design	"CAFs were derived from biopsy or surgical resection pieces of treatment-naive HNSCC patients operated in the Cliniques universitaires Saint-Luc (Brussels, Belgium), and patient-matched non-tumoral fibroblasts (NFs) were collected from a concomitant biopsy of the inner side of the cheek. CAFs were cultured in DMEM-GlutaMAX supplemented with 10% heat-inactivated FBS, 1% penicillin/streptomycin (Thermo Fisher Scientific) and 0.5 nM human recombinant basic fibroblast growth factor (b-FGF, Cat#78003, STEMCELL Technologies) and then maintained in exponential growth in 5% CO2/95% air in a humidified incubator at 37°C. NFs were grown in human fibroblast expansion basal medium (Cat#M106500, Gibco) supplemented with 2% low serum growth supplement (LSGS, Cat#S00310, Thermo Fisher Scientific) and 1% penicillin/streptomycin. The study was approved by the ethics committee from UCLouvain (approval ID UCL-ONCO 2015-02, Informed consent amendment 4, Version 5 dated on 04/03/2021) in accordance with the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki, and all patients gave their written informed consent."
